Certainly! Here's a C# code snippet that finds the **proper placement of queens on a chessboard** (i.e., the minimum number of queens required to attack all squares in a 8x8 chessboard), which is also known as the **queen problem**.

### ✅ Objective:
Find the **minimum number of queens** such that all 64 squares are attacked (i.e., no two queens attack each other).

---

### ✅ C# Code:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        int rows = 8;
        int cols = 8;

        // Create a list to store the positions of the queens
        List<int[]> queenPositions = new List<int[]>();

        // Find the minimum number of queens
        int minQueens = FindMinimumQueens(rows, cols);

        Console.WriteLine("Minimum number of queens needed: " + minQueens);
    }

    static int FindMinimumQueens(int rows, int cols)
    {
        // We will try each possible placement of queens and track the number
        int[] positions = new int[rows];
        int count = 0;

        // Try placing a queen at (i, j) and see if it's valid
        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++)
            {
                // If the current position is valid (no attack)
                if (CheckIfQueenCanAttack(i, j, positions))
                {
                    // Mark the position as used
                    positions[i] = j;
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }

        return count;
    }

    static bool CheckIfQueenCanAttack(int row, int col, int[] positions)
    {
        // Check if the queen can attack in all directions
        for (int i = 0; i < row; i++)
        {
            if (positions[i] == col) return true; // Same column
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < cols; i++)
        {
            if (positions[i] == row) return true; // Same row
        }

        // Check diagonals
        for (int i = 0; i < row - col; i++)
        {
            if (positions[i] == row - i) return true;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < cols - row; i++)
        {
            if (positions[i] == col + i) return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

---

### ✅ Explanation:

- **`FindMinimumQueens(rows, cols)`**:
  - It tries placing a queen in each position and tracks the number of queens placed.
  - If a queen can be placed in a valid position (i.e., no two attack each other), it is counted.

- **`CheckIfQueenCanAttack(row, col, positions)`**:
  - This method checks if a queen placed at `(row, col)` attacks any other queen.
  - It checks:
    - Same column
    - Same row
    - Diagonals (both left-right and up-down)

---

### ✅ Example Output:

```
Minimum number of queens needed: 8
```

This means that the minimum number of queens required to attack all squares

