To handle the logic where a `RepairItem` might have a `RepairOption` (or not) when updating, you can use the following code:

```php
public function update($values) {
    $model = RepairItem::find($id);

    if (Input::has('option')) {
        // Check if the option already exists
        $option = RepairOption::find($model->repairItem()->first());

        if ($option) {
            // Update the option
            $option->update($values);
            $model->fill(Input::except('option'));
        } else {
            // Create the option if it doesn't exist
            $option = RepairOption::create([
                'repair_item_id' => $model->id,
            ]);

            // Associate the option with the model
            $option->repairItem()->associate($model);

            // Save the option
            $option->save();

            // Update the model with the new values
            $model->fill($values);
        }
    } else {
        // Do nothing
        $model->update($values);
    }
}
```

This code ensures that if the `option` is provided, the model is updated (or created if needed), and it correctly associates the option with the model.

